  "overview": {
    "text": "Glucose is a simple sugar (a monosaccharide) with six carbon atoms and the formula C6H12O6. It is important because cells break it down during cellular respiration to make ATP, a usable form of energy. Plants make glucose and may join extra molecules into starch for storage; animals store glucose mainly as glycogen, while plants also use glucose units to build cellulose, a strong part of cell walls. In people, glucose carried in blood is called blood sugar: when its level rises, the pancreas releases insulin, which helps glucose enter cells. In water, glucose is usually in a ring-shaped form.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates",
      "https://medlineplus.gov/bloodglucose.html"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Glucose is a six-carbon sugar, or hexose, and its chemical formula is C6H12O6.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates",
        "https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/7-2-carbohydrates"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Glucose is a monosaccharide—the single-unit sugar that can serve as a building block for larger carbohydrates.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/7-2-carbohydrates"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Cells use energy released from glucose in cellular respiration to help make ATP.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Starch and glycogen store glucose; cellulose is made of glucose units and helps strengthen plant cell walls.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates",
        "https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/7-2-carbohydrates"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "When blood glucose rises, the pancreas releases insulin, which helps glucose move into cells for energy.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://medlineplus.gov/bloodglucose.html"
      ]
    }
  ],
